  <section id="best-for">
    <h2>Who Island Tower is best for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Two adults who want a small, newer room on the monorail.</strong> The Duo Studio is listed as one queen-size pull-down, sleeping two, at a lower rack range than a Tower Deluxe Studio.</li>
      <li><strong>Families who want a Theme Park View from the tower</strong> and will pay for it. Official listings include Magic Kingdom and Seven Seas Lagoon views on several categories.</li>
      <li><strong>People who want table-service dining in the same building.</strong> Wailulu Bar &amp; Grill is in the tower lobby, with indoor and outdoor seating.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Think twice if…</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You need a fifth bed in a studio.</strong> A Tower Deluxe Studio sleeps four. The longhouse Deluxe Studios on the <a href="/resorts/polynesian-villas-resort/">Polynesian Villas</a> page are the ones listed as sleeping five.</li>
      <li><strong>You are booking a dining reservation and plan to park at the tower.</strong> Official text says parking for Wailulu reservations is at the Polynesian Village hotel, not at Island Tower. Walk-up waitlist guests are told to park at the Transportation and Ticket Center and monorail or walk from there.</li>
      <li><strong>You assumed every table at Wailulu includes fireworks.</strong> Official text says Magic Kingdom nighttime entertainment is not guaranteed, some tables may not have a fireworks view, and specific seating requests are not accommodated.</li>
    </ul>
    <span class="ryan">[Ryan: who you think this resort is really for, from your own stays. Two or three sentences.]</span>
  </section>

